---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import HowItWorks from '../components/HowItWorks.astro';
import CardCabinet from '../components/CardCabinet.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick, REPO } from '../content/site';

const title = 'How hippo works: recall, decay and what we measured';
const description =
  "How hippo ranks, strengthens and fades an agent's memories, with the result of each test beside the mechanism. On synthetic tests two parts helped and decay made no measurable difference; sleep lowered recall on one scorer.";

const audit = 'https://github.com/kitfunso/hippo-memory/pull/232';
const decayResult = `${REPO}/blob/master/docs/evals/2026-09-24-decay-default-result.md`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="How it works" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro>
      <Fragment slot="title">How hippo decides what your agent remembers</Fragment>
      <p>
        Every memory has a strength. It fades on a half-life, grows each time the memory is recalled, and drops when you mark it wrong. The hippocampus inspired the design, and we tested each part on its own. On synthetic tests, outcome marks and recall strengthening helped and decay made no measurable difference. Sleep lowered recall on one scorer, and the rest is unmeasured.
      </p>
      <CardCabinet slot="figure" />
    </PageIntro>

    <HowItWorks />

    <Section title="Marking a recall good or bad" id="each-mechanism-with-what-was-measured" alt>
      <p>
        <code>hippo outcome --good</code> or <code>--bad</code> marks the memories from the last recall. After 5 good marks and no bad ones, a memory's half-life is about 42% longer. After 3 bad marks and no good ones it decays 1.6 times as fast. On a <a class="link" href={audit}>synthetic test</a> where every mark was correct, a bad mark cut how often a wrong memory stayed in the top five from 71.9% to 0.0%. Real marks are noisier, because <code>--bad</code> marks the whole recall batch.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Recall makes a memory last longer">
      <p>
        Each recall adds 2 days to a memory's half-life, so 365 days becomes 367 and then 369. Recall ranks memories by relevance, strength and recency, and the strongest matches fill the token budget first. In the first round of our audit this helped on the synthetic test, in its best case.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Errors last twice as long" alt>
      <p>
        A memory stored with <code>--error</code>, or captured from a failed tool call, gets a 730-day half-life against the usual 365. We haven't measured whether that helps.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Decay, on a 365-day half-life">
      <p>
        A memory's strength halves each time a half-life passes without use. The default used to be 7 days, which turned out to be a bug. On a <a class="link" href={decayResult}>test we registered first</a>, the current version of a fact was in the top five 29.2% of the time at 7 days and 74.7% at 365. We did not tune 365. It tied with 730 days and with decay switched off, so on that test decay made no measurable difference. Set <code>defaultHalfLifeDays</code> in <code>.hippo/config.json</code> to choose your own, or pin a memory so it never decays.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Sleep merges related memories" alt>
      <p>
        <code>hippo sleep</code> merges two or more related episodes into one pattern, and the originals fade. It keeps the store tidy. It has not been shown to improve recall, and on one scorer a slept store scored 3.6 points lower than the same store never slept. A memory that fades below the threshold goes dormant, <code>hippo dormant restore &lt;id&gt;</code> brings it back, and a dormant memory nobody restores is deleted after 180 days by default.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Replacing a fact, and spotting contradictions">
      <p>
        <code>hippo supersede &lt;id&gt; "&lt;new fact&gt;"</code> stores the new version and links the old one to it. The old one leaves recall and stays on record, and <code>hippo recall --as-of &lt;date&gt;</code> shows what was current on a given day. <code>hippo reject &lt;id&gt; --reason "&lt;why&gt;"</code> stops a value coming back at all. When two memories overlap in content and contradict each other, hippo records a conflict that <code>hippo conflicts</code> lists. Each memory also carries a confidence level of verified, observed, inferred or stale. None of this is measured yet.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq
      items={faqPick(['Is this just RAG?'])}
      heading="Questions about how it works"
      moreHref="/faq/"
    />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
